  "answer": "It can raise an issue, but I cannot predict the outcome for your particular application. The supplied passages identify two separate concerns.\n\n1. Distinctiveness — Manual practice. The examiner focuses on the identifier within the web address, rather than its standard address components. If the mark consists only of standard address code material and an identifier that other traders would wish to use in the normal course of trade, the Manual says it is not prima facie capable of distinguishing. The relevant question is therefore not simply whether the word is common, but whether other traders would wish to use it in their trade. [TMM/Part22/14/14/2~1]\n\nOwning or being authorised to use the domain name does not, by itself, overcome a section 41 objection. Under the Manual’s guidance, evidence that use has given the domain name a secondary meaning indicating trade origin may help; use alone does not necessarily establish distinctiveness. The supplied legislation does not include section 41, so this is an account of Manual practice, not the complete statutory test. [TMM/Part22/14/14/2~2]\n\n2. Domain-name entitlement — Manual practice. Separately, the Manual says a section 43 objection applies where the mark is likely to be viewed as an internet domain name but the applicant is not its registrant or authorised user. That objection will be reconsidered if ownership or authorised use is established. [TMM/Part29/8#1]\n\nThe law — Act, section 43. The statutory ground is that a connotation of the mark makes its use for the particular goods or services likely to deceive or cause confusion. The domain-name guidance above is the Manual’s application of that provision, not the wording of the Act itself. [TMA1995/s43; TMM/Part29/8#1]"
